7592186045417] [17592186180012 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)F \\cdot dr$, where $F(x, y) = \\cos xi + \\sin yj$ and $C$ is the curve $y = 2x^{2} - 1$ traversed from the point $A(1, 1)$ to the point $B(2, 7)$?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t) = ti + (2t^{2} - 1)j$.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180012 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d4e0663c-1824-56f9-b923-60eb7cd3a338" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180015 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180015 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124936" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180015 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate $∮_(C)F(r) \\cdot dr$, where $F(x, y) = yi - xj$ and $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180015 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a8909b1-1a48-5c56-ac06-8b954196fc3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180172 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180172 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39341" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180172 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/48/4807f92defb4202359a32ca046073cd138db396a0975164e42b96126ac0cc591.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)F \\cdot dr$, where $F(x, y) = e^{x}i + e^{-2y}j$ and $C$ is the arc of a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin traversed counterclockwise from the point $(1, 0)$ to the point $(0, 1)$?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t) = \\cos ti + \\sin tj$.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180172 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e19e4fc2-d0da-5e51-a13e-b5b2b6e03a48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180488 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180488 #edb/ref 1061 35690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180488 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7b85eebf-c70c-58a0-9476-5bcb73727ad3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180488 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180489 #edb/ref 1061 35691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180489 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c535e9dc-5505-59aa-ae55-5a85fcb5135c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180489 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180490 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180490 #edb/ref 1061 35692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180490 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "922dce6d-b939-516c-95ed-a47bcba850e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180490 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180491 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180491 #edb/ref 1061 35693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180491 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2bd70267-cee5-535e-880d-6ade2c843224" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180491 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180718 #edb/ref 1064 "The line integral of a vector-valued function $\\mathbf F$ along a piecewise smooth curve $C$ is denoted\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r .\n$$\n\nThis integral represents the total work done by $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle from an initial point $A$ to a terminal point $B$ along the curve.\n\nIf the curve can be parameterized by the function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ then we can calculate the line integral using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\textrm dt\n$$\n\nOften, the trickiest part is finding a parameterization for $C.$ In this lesson, we'll consider several different cases" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180718 #edb/ref 1065 5823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180718 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ec010a30-571c-5ad5-b30a-c18ffdd997e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180718 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
